{
  "dom_id": "step-q315610",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14195525/assets/d85a061ab65d269f22f589e7f57b07a6d474db5f6f29c8c8b412701b99de5241.png)\n\nA box has a square base with side lengths $x$ and height $y,$ as shown above. The surface area of the box is $294$ square units. Find the value of $x$ that maximizes the volume of the box. Do not round the answer.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}} units",
  "worked_solution": "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV={x}^{2}y\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The surface area is $294$ square units. So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\,\\text{(area of the base)}+4\\,\\text{(area of one side)} \\\\ 294 & =2{x}^{2}+4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{147}{2x}-\\frac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=\\frac{147}{2x}-\\frac{x}{2}$ in the expression $V={x}^{2}y,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(x) & ={x}^{2}(\\frac{147}{2x}-\\frac{x}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{147}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(x)=\\frac{147}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{147}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =49 \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the last step, we only considered the positive root because the side length $x$ must be positive. \n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $x=7$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(x)=-3x\\,⟹\\,{V}^{″}(7)=-21<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(7)$ is negative, we conclude that $x=7$ is indeed a maximum of $V(x).$\n\nTherefore, the box with the largest possible volume has side length $x=7$ units.",
